Barnaby was not your average house cat. While his siblings spent their days napping in sunbeams and their nights staring blankly at walls, Barnaby possessed a spirit of restless curiosity. He was a tuxedo cat with eyes the color of polished emeralds, and he had a secret: he knew how to open the back door.

It was a Tuesday afternoon, the kind of gray, drizzly day that made the world feel small and safe. But Barnaby felt restless. The house smelled of lavender laundry detergent and boredom. He padded silently to the kitchen, his paws making no sound on the linoleum. With a practiced twist of his paw, he nudged the latch of the back door. It clicked. The door swung open just enough for him to slip through.

The air outside was thick with the scent of wet earth and pine needles. Barnaby stepped onto the patio, his tail twitching with anticipation. He didn’t go far—just to the edge of the garden where the manicured lawn gave way to the wilder, tangled edge of the woods. But today, the wind was stronger, carrying a scent he had never smelled before: something metallic, sharp, and exciting.

He followed the scent, weaving through the tall grass. Suddenly, a rustle in the bushes made him freeze. His ears swiveled like radar dishes. Out popped a squirrel, but not just any squirrel. This one was wearing a tiny, bright red scarf knitted from yarn.

Barnaby blinked. Squirrels didn’t wear scarves.

The squirrel chattered, dropping a shiny, silver object at Barnaby’s feet before darting up an oak tree. Barnaby approached the object cautiously. It was a bell, small and silver, with a tiny tag attached that read: *Lost & Found: The Moonlight Market.*

Curiosity got the better of him. He picked up the bell in his teeth and followed the trail of red yarn that seemed to lead deeper into the woods. The trees grew thicker, the canopy above blocking out the gray sky. The air grew cooler, and the sounds of the suburban neighborhood faded away, replaced by the hoot of an owl and the rustle of leaves.

